<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD JATS (Z39.96) Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v1.0 20120330//EN" "JATS-archivearticle1.dtd"><article article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Br J Cancer</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="iso-abbrev">Br. J. Cancer</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>British Journal of Cancer</journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="ppub">0007-0920</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1532-1827</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Nature Publishing Group</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">10070895</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2362649</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pii">6690151</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1038/sj.bjc.6690151</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Regular Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Comparison of the Ki-67 score and S-phase fraction as prognostic variables in soft-tissue sarcoma</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Huuhtanen</surname><given-names>R L</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Blomqvist</surname><given-names>C P</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Wiklund</surname><given-names>T A</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>B&#x000f6;hling</surname><given-names>T O</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff2">2</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Virolainen</surname><given-names>M J</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff2">2</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Tukiainen</surname><given-names>E J</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff3">3</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Tribukait</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff4">4</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Andersson</surname><given-names>L C</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff2">2</xref></contrib><aff id="aff1">Department of Oncology, Helsinki University Central Hospital, Helsinki, FIN-00029-HYKS, Finland</aff><aff id="aff2">Department of Pathology, University of Helsinki, Haartman Institute, S-17176-Stockholm, Sweden</aff><aff id="aff3">Helsinki University Central Hospital, Department of Plastic Surgery, S-17176-Stockholm, Sweden;</aff><aff id="aff4">Karolinska Institute and Hospital,Department of Radiology, S-17176-Stockholm, Sweden</aff></contrib-group><author-notes><corresp id="caf1"><label>*</label>Helsinki University Central Hospital,Department of Oncology, PO Box 180, FIN-00029-HYKS, Finland</corresp></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>2</month><year>1999</year></pub-date><volume>79</volume><issue>5-6</issue><fpage>945</fpage><lpage>951</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>04</day><month>02</month><year>1998</year></date><date date-type="rev-recd"><day>18</day><month>05</month><year>1998</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>29</day><month>05</month><year>1998</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 1999 Cancer Research Campaign</copyright-statement><copyright-year>1999</copyright-year><copyright-holder>Cancer Research Campaign</copyright-holder></permissions><abstract><p>Immunohistochemically determined Ki-67 scores and flow cytometrically determined S-phase fractions were successfully evaluated from the primary tumours of 123 patients with soft-tissue sarcoma. All patients had either limb or superficial trunk tumours. Ki-67 score correlated strongly with ploidy, S-phase fraction and grade. Ki-67 did not correlate with the size of the primary tumour. When analysed as a continuous variable, Ki-67 was a stronger predictor of both metastasis-free survival and disease-specific overall survival (<italic>P</italic>= 0.003 and 0.04 respectively) than was the S-phase fraction (<italic>P</italic>= 0.06 and 0.07 respectively). We tested the relevance of different cut-point values by dividing the whole material into two parts at every 10&#x00025; (e.g. 10&#x00025; of patients vs. the remaining 90&#x00025;, 20&#x00025; vs. 80&#x00025;, etc.). We counted the relative risk and confidence interval at all these cut-off points. Ki-67 had good prognostic discriminating power irrespective of the cut-point value, but S-phase fraction lost its prognostic power at higher cut-point values. In conclusion, we found that Ki-67 is a useful prognostic tool in the treatment of soft-tissue sarcoma patients irrespective of the cut-point value. S-phase fraction can be used at lower cut-point values. <italic>&#x000a9; 1999 Cancer Research Campaign</italic></p></abstract><kwd-group><kwd>soft-tissue sarcoma</kwd><kwd>prognosis</kwd><kwd>S-phase</kwd><kwd>Ki-67</kwd><kwd>flow cytometry</kwd><kwd>immunohistochemistry</kwd></kwd-group></article-meta></front></article>